U.S. Fed cuts key interest rate
The U.S. Federal Reserve cut a key interest rate by half-a-percentage point to 1 percent Wednesday in an effort to spur economic activity.
The cut put the central bank's federal funds rate at 1 percent
matching its lowest ever level.
The last time it was at 1 percent was from June 2003 to June 2004.
The rate is used to set rates for a wide variety of consumer loans, including credit cards, and many business loans.
and the second this month after an emergency cut on October 8
as it grapples with problems in the U.S. economy.
