Airplane crash-lands into Hudson River;
all aboard reported safe
A US Airways plane with more than 150 people aboard went down in the Hudson River on Thursday after taking off from LaGuardia Airport, and everyone aboard apparently got off the plane alive, officials said.
Flight 1549, headed to Charlotte, North Carolina, may have experienced a bird strike, according to FAA spokeswoman Laura Brown.
Passenger Alberto Pinero said that within a couple minutes after takeoff, "you just heard a loud bang and the plane shook a bit."
"Somehow, the plane stayed afloat and we were all able to get on a raft," Pinero said. "It's just incredible now that everyone's still alive."
The plane had 148 passengers, Brown said, and either five or six crew members on board when it took off at 3:26 p.m.
It was airborne for less than three minutes, she said.
Everyone on board is believed to have exited the Airbus A320, the Federal Aviation Administration said.
The Coast Guard rescued 35 people, according to Coast Guard Cmdr. Ron LeBrec.
Roosevelt Hospital said it had received four passengers who were being treated for hypothermia, but had no further injures.
Other hospitals were on alert.
The temperature in New York was 20 degrees about the time of the crash off Manhattan's west side.
Witnesses reported seeing the plane hit a flock of birds, Brown said.
