Despite high oil prices and an ascending won against the dollar, June exports hit a monthly record of $28.27 billion, up 19.2 percent from a year ago, the Commerce Ministry said.
The ministry attributed the surge of exports to the steady growth of the global economy, domestic pressure to cut inventories, and concerns over aggravated export conditions in the latter half of the year.
The Korea Trade and Investment Promotion Agency, however, projected export growth of 11 percent year-on-year in the second half of the year, slowing from an estimated 14.4 percent rise in the first six months of the year.
The agency predicted that this year's exports would top $320 billion.
