South Korea´s economic growth is expected to slow to 4.6 percent next year, from an expected 4.8 percent expansion this year, amid global credit worries related to U.S. subprime mortgage loans, an official at the International Monetary Fund said Thursday.
“A modest slowdown, from about 4.8 percent this year to 4.6 percent in 2008, is expected with a sharper-than expected U.S. slowdown the key risk,” said Jerald Schiff, the assistant director of Asia-Pacific Department at the IMF, in a breakfast meeting in Seoul.
”We estimate that a 1 percentage point fall in the U.S. growth rate could still lead to a 0.25 to 0.5 percent decline in Korea,” Schiff said.
South Korean officials have played down the impact of the U.S. subprime crisis, saying the nation´s economic fundamentals are strong.
