Leaders of 15 European nations that use the Euro have come up with a tentative plan to help unfreeze the credit market.
The plan, they hope, will stop the stock market meltdown.
Meeting in Paris today, they have all decided to guarantee future bank debts for five years.
The idea is to get banks to trust each other again so they'll start lending to each other, and then to people in businesses who need cash.
They haven't officially announced this yet; only written it in a draft statement obtained by the Associated Press.
Former Treasury Secretary Larry Summer says this is just what's making the current U.S. economic crisis worse, than the one that caused the stock market crash of 1987.
“He didn't have the breakdown in all the trading between the banks.
