Merajuddin Patan, the governor of Ghazni Province, where the 23 hostages _ two of whom were later killed by the kidnappers _ were seized by Taliban militants, said there were hopes that the crisis would be resolved without further loss of lives.
The governor said the Taliban were not asking for a ransom but were demanding the release of prisoners from Ghazni Province and now from other provinces as well.
Meanwhile, a purported Taliban spokesman said on Wednesday that the insurgents still want negotiations despite the expiry of the latest deadline.
Qari Yousuf Ahmadi said that the remaining 21 hostages are still alive for the time being, denying an earlier report that the militant group would kill four more captives as Kabul refused to accept its demand to free jailed guerrilla fighters.
