The list was part of a deal in October under which North Korea agreed to end its nuclear program in exchange for aid and diplomatic concessions.
Hill has spent part of his visit touring the Yongbyon nuclear facility.
The US Assistant Secretary of State is the highest-profile foreign diplomat to have visited the ageing nuclear complex, north of the capital, which houses North Korea´s only operational reactor.
US experts are supervising the removal of 8,000 spent fuel rods as part of an international deal, first agreed in February and confirmed in October, under which North Korea ends its nuclear program in return for economic aid and diplomatic concessions.
