China claims that Tibetan ""independence forces"" are planning to launch suicide attacks as part of a wider uprising to establish an independent Tibet.
Public Security Ministry spokesman Wu Heping said these suicide squads ""fear neither bloodshed nor sacrifice"".
Wu did not produce any evidence to back up the claims - but said China would release proof later.
The prime minister of the Tibetan government-in-exile, Samdhong Rinpoche, denied China´s allegations.
Wu claimed the wave of protests that erupted across Tibetan areas recently was part of a ""Tibetan People´s Uprising Movement"" ahead of the Olympic Games.
The issue of whether to allow North Korea to develop a civilian nuclear power program is not likely to be an obstacle that would derail the six-party talks on the North's nuclear arms program, the top U.S. envoy to the talks said in Washington.
