Dozens of gay couples rushed to tie the knot at chapels, parks and beaches across Massachusetts on Thursday as the end of the three-day waiting period under state matrimonial law led to a marathon of same-sex weddings.
Massachusetts law establishes a three-day waiting period between applying for a marriage license and getting married.
However, dozens of couples obtained a court waiver of the waiting period and got married promptly after Massachusetts on Monday became the first state to allow same-sex couples to wed.
The new round of nuptials came as Gov. Mitt Romney took the first steps toward blocking town clerks from issuing licenses to out-of-state gay couples, which the Republican governor claims is prohibited by state law.
