Fewer Koreans think they belong to the middle class than three years ago, feeling pushed down to the lower rungs of the social ladder.
Koreans were generally not happy, with only three out of 10 people saying they were content with their lives.
According to the National Statistical Office (NSO), which surveyed members of about 33,000 households across the nation, 53.4 percent said they belong to the middle class in social and economic terms, when considering income, profession, education and assets.
The ratio was 56.2 percent in the survey of 2003.
The survey is conducted every three years.
Those who thought they belonged to a lower class made up 45.2 percent, rising 2.8 percentage points from 2003.
Only 1.46 percent of households said they were upper class.
