˝Next to the calories and nutrition details on the side of a food package, you may soon be looking at the words “China-Free,” as the labeling movement that debuted in the U.S. is quietly trickling into South Korean food and beverage industries.
The move, which was first triggered in June by a Utah-based health food company for consumer awareness, has stirred controversy that it may promote racism.
But a rising number of American businesses, consumers and interest groups are welcoming the labeling method as Chinese food and other imports have recently been headline news for their unacceptable health and safety standards.
An online poll by U.S. news channel MSBNC showed that 77 percent of 9,865 people said they were in favor of the ``China-Free´´ labeling since an at-a-glance sticker tells shoppers what they need to know right away, while only 7.7 percent said they were against it.
