South Korea´s central bank is expected to stand pat on the key interest rate for the fourth straight month in December to bolster economic growth amid volatile local financial markets despite growing inflationary pressures, a poll showed Tuesday.
Economists from 12 financial institutions unanimously predicted that the Bank of Korea (BOK) will freeze the December target for the call rate at a six-year high of 5 percent on Friday, the poll by Yonhap Infomax, the financial news arm of Yonhap News Agency, showed.
The poll was conducted from Nov.
The central bank has frozen the call rate, the interest charged on overnight inter-bank loans, since raising it by a quarter point each in July and August to curb rising liquidity, which could fuel inflation and an asset bubble.
