For more than two decades, about 10 petrochemical companies have colluded to rig prices and turn profits unfairly, a top-ranking official at Korea's antitrust watchdog said yesterday.
The companies are expected to pay 200 billion won or about $213 million in fines.
The Fair Trade Commission official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said, “a probe of petrochemical companies has confirmed that they have rigged prices . . . of synthetic resin products, including polyethylene, since the late 1970s.”
Synthetic resins refer to materials used to make vinyl and various kitchen containers.
