Officials in Seoul expressed disappointment at Washington's refusal to accept their request.
At the last round of negotiations, in Montana this month, Korea pushed U.S. officials to limit the scope of duties it could put on products such as steel and semiconductors.
The United States rejected those changes then, and with a filing with Congress on Wednesday, pledged to Congress that it won't sign-off on any types of legal changes as part of an overall free-trade deal.
Korea wants the United States to make concessions in antidumping laws that Seoul believes are unfair for its exporters, such as Samsung Electronics Co. and Hynix Semiconductor Inc., to move forward negotiations for a proposed FTA.
Despite U.S. refusal, Korea's Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade said in a statement it will continue to press the U.S. side.
