The Korean won is forecast to gain further against the dollar this year on globally weak dollar sentiment amid the record-high U.S. fiscal and current account deficits, analysts said.
They also said the strengthening yuan will push up the value of the won and the communist Chinese central bank’s plan to diversify its foreign exchange holdings into the euro and other currencies from the dollar will make the dollar even weaker on the global stage.
The government has pledged to further ease rules on investment in overseas securities and real estate to boost capital outflow from the country.
But these steps are expected to have limits in curbing the won’s strength as long as global dollar sentiment remains weak, analysts said.
