The British physicist Stephen Hawking has completed a zero-gravity flight in a specially modified plane.
Professor Hawking, who suffers from motor neuron disease, was able to float free, unrestricted by his paralyzed muscles and his wheelchair.
The flight, which lasted more than an hour took a series of dramatic dives, allowing the professor to experience 25-second spurts of weightlessness.
The event could be a step closer to Hawking’s goal of going into space.
The modified Boeing 727 jet simulates the experience of weightlessness as it takes plunges over the Atlantic Ocean.
