The move also suspends work at a medical facility inside the camp, where Iraqis came for treatment.
About 460 South Korean medics and military engineers have been in Nasiriyah for almost a year.
They will come home after South Korea sends the new deployment of up to 3,600 troops to the Kurdish region of northern Iraq later this year.
Acting President Goh Kun said Wednesday that South Korea will push ahead with its troops dispatch.
