Further inciting political instability on the Korean Peninsula, US President George W. Bush revived his famous description of Iran and North Korea on Friday as part of ""an axis of evil"" as they cause the same security threat because of their lack of transparency and pursuit of weapons of mass destruction.
It was the first time for Bush to use the incendiary phrase ""axis of evil"" during his second term.
He first introduced the concept in his State of the Union address in 2002 to describe the security threat posed by Iran, North Korea and Iraq.
The embassy admitted it now takes longer for applicants to get visas.
A long queue of people wait impatiently outside the U.S.
The Korea Times reports since the introduction of a new U.S. visa application system on Aug. 2, over 95 percent of applicants have been required to appear for a non-immigrant visa interview.
And this complicated new process is leaving applicants bewildered and upset.
Previously, the American Embassy exempted those between the ages of 55 and 80 from visa interviews, resulting in only about 65 percent of applicants needing to go through the interview process. Currently, less than 5 percent of Koreans are exempt from visa interviews.
They include diplomatic or official passport holders and their dependents, children younger than 14 and adults aged 80 or older.
