The opposition Grand National Party is preparing a North Korean human rights bill that would require the government to put aside funds for humanitarian aid to the North, and to investigate the fate of defectors who are forcibly returned to North Korea.
Unlike comparable laws in the United States and Japan, however, the bill does not include provisions for imposing sanctions on North Korea.
South Korea will give rice aid to North Korea even if Pyongyang misses an imminent deadline to shut down a key nuclear plant, a top official has said.
Vice Unification Minister Shin Eon-sang said that while aid and the nuclear freeze remained linked, “either of them can come half a step earlier”.
North Korea asked Seoul for 400,000 ton s of rice aid at talks last month. The comments came a day after top Asian diplomats voiced doubt that Pyongyang would meet the April 14th deadline.
