Bad news for George W. Americans are anxious about the economy and unhappy about the Iraq war.
The number of soldiers killed will probably hit the psychologically important 1,000 mark before the November election. And in the political horse race, he is tied or worse with Democratic rival John Kerry.
There's a strong market for change at the White House, lifting Democrats' hopes.
Kerry enters his convention stronger than Ronald Reagan in 1980 or Bill Clinton in 1992, the last two challengers to an incumbent president, though Bush's side argues that he is rebounding.
