Seoul appellant court ruled that migrant workers have the right to organize a labor union regardless of their legal status.
The Seoul High Court overturned a lower court ruling which backed the government's refusal of a request by a group of foreign workers to form a labor union.
Foreign workers welcomed the ruing as paving the way for the better protection of labor rights of more than 360,000 migrant workers, some of whom face rampant abuse and unfair treatment at their workplace.
In May 2005, the Seoul-Gyeonggi-Incheon Migrant Trade Union submitted an application to launch a legal labor union at a Seoul regional office of the Labor Ministry.
